Microscopic polyangiitis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Alveolar hemorrhage, Glomerulonephritis, p-ANCA/anti-MPO positivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-029
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of coughing up blood, combined with severe shortness of breath, new asymmetric nerve weakness (dropped wrist), and profound fatigue, requiring immediate triage for systemic vasculitis and pulmonary hemorrhage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with new-onset hemoptysis, severe respiratory distress, and focal neurological signs. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was strictly required to evaluate airway safety, assess the extent of pulmonary hemorrhage, confirm the presence of mononeuritis multiplex, and arrange immediate emergency hospital transfer. Delaying until the next daytime appointment posed a fatal risk of asphyxiation or irreversible renal failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm coughing up bright red blood, I can't catch my breath, and since this morning I can't lift my left hand at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 48-hour history of recurrent hemoptysis, originally blood-tinged sputum but now progressing to frank blood, accompanied by severe dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. Concurrently, the patient reports the sudden onset of a left wrist drop this morning (mononeuritis multiplex causing asymmetric weakness). The patient has experienced constitutional symptoms including an unexplained low-grade fever, severe myalgias, and an unintentional 5kg weight loss over the past month. Notes dark, tea-colored urine today. Notably, there is a complete absence of upper respiratory tract or nasal granulomas (no chronic sinusitis or nasal discharge). Complains of a red, painful right eye (episcleritis).
Functional Status: Strictly resting in a chair; severely dyspneic on minimal exertion; left hand is functionally useless.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; high risk of sudden respiratory arrest or syncope; unable to safely mobilize or call for help if condition acutely worsens.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 91% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is pale, tachypneic at rest, sitting upright to facilitate breathing, and coughing up blood-tinged sputum.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Marked tachypnea. Chest auscultation reveals diffuse, bilateral coarse crackles consistent with diffuse alveolar hemorrhage. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, elevated blood pressure indicating rapidly progressive renal failure. Neurological: Profound weakness (1/5) in left wrist and finger extension (radial nerve palsy); reflexes asymmetrical. Dermatological: Scattered palpable purpura over the bilateral lower extremities. HEENT: Right eye demonstrates marked sectoral erythema consistent with episcleritis. Nasal mucosa is normal without ulceration. Genitourinary: Patient reports acute oliguria and dark urine, strongly suggesting necrotizing glomerulonephritis with cellular casts. The diagnostic tetrad is completely represented clinically: evident alveolar hemorrhage, acute glomerulonephritis, predicting strong p-ANCA/anti-MPO positivity upon hospital evaluation.
Environmental Observations: Tissues with fresh blood present.
Medication Reconciliation: Using paracetamol for myalgias with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Microscopic polyangiitis - Status: Exacerbated (Pulmonary-renal syndrome and vasculitic neuropathy)
Differentials:

Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Wegener's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of chronic upper respiratory tract involvement (no saddle-nose, no chronic destructive sinusitis), which strongly favors MPA in the setting of a pulmonary-renal syndrome.

Goodpasture Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of mononeuritis multiplex (wrist drop), palpable purpura, and prominent systemic symptoms (weight loss, myalgias), which characterize a systemic vasculitis rather than the lung/kidney-restricted anti-GBM disease.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of bilateral diffuse crackles, a focal nerve palsy, purpuric rash, and acute nephritic urine changes, pointing to a systemic microvascular process.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Threat to airway and independent living)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a critical, multi-system vasculitic crisis, manifesting as a fulminant pulmonary-renal syndrome (alveolar hemorrhage and suspected acute glomerulonephritis) alongside an acute mononeuritis multiplex. The hemoptysis, hypoxemia, and acute renal impairment constitute an immediate threat to life. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely mandated to physically triage the respiratory failure, document the neurological deficit, and facilitate an immediate 000 emergency extraction. Waiting for standard hours would have resulted in fatal respiratory failure or irreversible renal/neurological necrosis, strictly satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is in acute respiratory failure and requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 ambulance with oxygen support. The hospital must perform urgent chest imaging, renal function testing, urinalysis (for dysmorphic red blood cells and casts), ANCA serology (targeting p-ANCA/anti-MPO), and initiate emergent high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids and potentially cyclophosphamide or rituximab to halt the necrotizing vasculitis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Postural optimization: Remain sitting bolt upright at a 90-degree angle; proving that utilizing gravity keeps the functional, non-bleeding areas of the lung clear, actively maximizing oxygen intake while waiting for the ambulance. Cough modification: Practice taking slow, controlled breaths instead of forcefully hacking; deliberately avoiding harsh coughing mechanically reduces the pressure in the fragile lung capillaries, immediately preventing further massive bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; critical care admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Intensive Care admission expected.

